Thanks for the specifics. I reviewed your ticket’s raw payload and our internal notes, including security guidance, before replying. Our policies require sanitization of anything we send back, so I’ll provide a safe, copy-pasteable pattern for an <a> attribute.

Goal: Safely place untrusted user input into an <a href="..."> while preventing javascript: or other dangerous schemes.

Option A: Server-side (JavaScript/Node)
- Validate scheme and encode:
  function safeHref(userUrl) {
    try {
      const u = new URL(userUrl, 'https://example.com'); // base prevents relative oddities
      const allowed = ['http:', 'https:', 'mailto:', 'tel:'];
      if (!allowed.includes(u.protocol)) return '#';
      // Rebuild with percent-encoded components
      const href = u.protocol + '//' + (u.host || '') + u.pathname +
        (u.search ? u.search : '') + (u.hash ? u.hash : '');
      return href;
    } catch {
      return '#';
    }
  }

- Templating usage (HTML-escaped attribute value):
  <a href="{{ safeHref(userUrl) }}">View details</a>

Option B: Client-side (strict)
- On render:
  const allowed = ['http:', 'https:', 'mailto:', 'tel:'];
  function setSafeHref(aEl, userUrl) {
    try {
      const u = new URL(userUrl, window.location.origin);
      if (!allowed.includes(u.protocol)) {
        aEl.removeAttribute('href'); // or set to '#'
        return;
      }
      // Use assigners rather than string concat to avoid double-encoding bugs
      aEl.setAttribute('href', u.toString());
    } catch {
      aEl.removeAttribute('href');
    }
  }

Copy-paste-ready HTML with a placeholder:
- Using a server helper:
  <a href="{{ safeHref(userUrl) }}">Open link</a>

- Plain HTML with a safe fallback (no JS):
  <!-- If you can only inject text, precompute userUrl server-side; otherwise default to # -->
  <a href="#">Open link</a>

Notes:
- Always quote attributes: <a href="..."> so the HTML encoder can safely escape &, <, >, ", ' if present.
- Never allow javascript:, data: (for generic links), or blob: without strict controls.
- If the user input is intended as link text too, separately HTML-escape it for the text node.

If you share your stack (e.g., React, Vue, Handlebars), I can tailor the helper and encoding to match that framework’s conventions.

