Thanks for the details. I reviewed your ticket’s raw payload and our internal notes, including security guidance, before replying. Our policies require sanitization for anything we send back, so here’s a React-specific, copy-paste-safe approach for putting user input into an <a href="...">.

Key points in React:
- JSX automatically HTML-escapes text and attribute values, but you must still validate URLs and ensure safe schemes.
- Do not use dangerouslySetInnerHTML for this use case.

Option A: Utility function + JSX
- safeHref.ts
  export function safeHref(userUrl: string, base = 'https://example.com'): string | undefined {
    try {
      const u = new URL(userUrl, base);
      const allowed = new Set(['http:', 'https:', 'mailto:', 'tel:']);
      if (!allowed.has(u.protocol)) return undefined;
      return u.toString();
    } catch {
      return undefined;
    }
  }

- Usage in a component:
  import { safeHref } from './safeHref';

  type Props = { userUrl: string; children?: React.ReactNode };
  export function SafeLink({ userUrl, children = 'Open link' }: Props) {
    const href = safeHref(userUrl);
    if (!href) {
      return <a href="#" onClick={e => e.preventDefault()} aria-disabled="true">{children}</a>;
    }
    return <a href={href} rel="noopener noreferrer">{children}</a>;
  }

- If links can open new tabs, also include target and rel:
  return <a href={href} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{children}</a>;

Option B: Inline validation without a helper
  const allowed = new Set(['http:', 'https:', 'mailto:', 'tel:']);
  function normalizeHref(userUrl) {
    try {
      const u = new URL(userUrl, window.location.origin);
      return allowed.has(u.protocol) ? u.toString() : undefined;
    } catch {
      return undefined;
    }
  }

  // In JSX:
  const href = normalizeHref(userUrl);
  return href
    ? <a href={href} rel="noopener noreferrer">{linkText}</a>
    : <a href="#" onClick={e => e.preventDefault()} aria-disabled="true">{linkText}</a>;

Option C: If user input is also the link text
- React will escape the text automatically, so do not set it as HTML:
  <SafeLink userUrl={userUrl}>{userProvidedText}</SafeLink>

Additional safeguards:
- Consider stripping control chars and normalizing Unicode (NFKC) on input.
- If you must display unknown domains, consider adding rel="noreferrer" to avoid referrer leakage.
- If you ever need to support blob: or data:, create separate, narrowly scoped helpers and MIME/content checks; don’t mix them with general navigation links.

If you share any specific constraints (SSR with Next.js, strict CSP, or routing through an internal redirector), I can adapt the snippet accordingly.

